menu "app_mqtt -- broker link (optional)"

config APP_MQTT_ENABLE
    bool "Enable the MQTT client"
    default n
    help
        Off by default, and it should be: it needs a broker that may not exist, and
        nothing else in the template depends on it. PLAN §8 lists it as an extra.

        What it is actually for is pushing "check for an update now" to a whole fleet at
        once, instead of waiting out each device's jittered six-hour poll.

config APP_MQTT_BROKER_URI
    string "Broker URI"
    default "mqtts://nas.lan:8883"
    depends on APP_MQTT_ENABLE
    help
        mqtts:// for TLS, which is what the broker in nas/docker-compose.yml serves and
        what you should use. The host must then appear in the server certificate's SAN
        list, exactly as with the OTA base URL.

        mqtt:// also works, for an existing broker on a LAN you trust. Be clear about what
        it costs: the username and password below cross the network in the clear on every
        connect, and so does every command and every status message. The password is
        already weak protection -- it is compiled into the image and shared by the whole
        fleet -- and without TLS it is barely protection at all, so anyone on that network
        can publish commands to your devices. The firmware logs a warning at boot when the
        link is unencrypted rather than letting it pass unremarked.

        The shipped broker has no 1883 listener, because a plaintext port on a broker you
        control gets used "just for testing" and then stays.

config APP_MQTT_USERNAME
    string "Broker username"
    default "esp-ota"
    depends on APP_MQTT_ENABLE

config APP_MQTT_PASSWORD
    string "Broker password"
    default ""
    depends on APP_MQTT_ENABLE
    help
        From tools/gen_mqtt_user.sh. This is build-time configuration shared by every
        device on the broker, not a per-device secret -- anyone who can read an image can
        read it. Acceptable because what the broker controls is "check now", and the
        update itself is signed and verified independently (PLAN §5). If MQTT ever
        carries something that matters more, move to per-device client certificates.

        Empty means the client connects with no credentials, which the shipped
        mosquitto.conf refuses.

config APP_MQTT_TOPIC_PREFIX
    string "Topic prefix"
    default "esp-ota"
    depends on APP_MQTT_ENABLE
    help
        Topics are <prefix>/<device-id>/{status,online,cmd} plus <prefix>/all/cmd for the
        whole fleet. Set it to the project name so one broker can carry several projects,
        the same way one NAS carries several projects' firmware.

config APP_MQTT_STATUS_INTERVAL_S
    int "Seconds between status publishes"
    default 60
    range 5 3600
    depends on APP_MQTT_ENABLE
    help
        Status is also published immediately on connect and whenever something
        interesting changes, so this is the heartbeat rather than the only signal.

config APP_MQTT_KEEPALIVE_S
    int "MQTT keepalive, seconds"
    default 60
    range 10 600
    depends on APP_MQTT_ENABLE
    help
        How long the broker waits before declaring the device gone and publishing its
        last will. Too high and a dashboard shows a dead device as online for minutes;
        too low and a device on poor Wi-Fi flaps.

config APP_MQTT_ALLOW_RESTART
    bool "Accept restart commands from the broker"
    default y
    depends on APP_MQTT_ENABLE
    help
        Anyone who can publish to the command topic can reboot every device at once.
        That is the same trade the web UI already makes (PLAN §7) and the broker at least
        requires a password -- but a fleet-wide reboot is a bigger blast radius than one
        board, so it is worth being able to compile out while leaving "check now" alone.

endmenu
